Power JSON (<code>example:data</code>)Example <code>.mcfunction</code> file <pre><code>{
    "type": "neo-apoli:nbt"
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>#   Set the contents of the 'example:data' power
data merge neo-apoli:power @s example:data {value: "Hello world!", cool_items: [{id: "minecraft:egg", count: 1}, {id: "minecraft:diamond", count: 1}]}

#   Count how many elements are in the 'cool_items' NBT of the 'example:data' power
execute store result score #stacks example if data neo-apoli:power @s example:data cool_items[]

#   Display how many item stacks are in the 'cool_items' NBT of the 'example:data' power
tellraw @a ["There are ", {"score": {"name": "#stacks", "objective": "example"}}, " cool item stack(s)!"]
</code></pre> <p>This example provides a power to be used as a storage for NBT data and a function file that would set and display some of its contents.</p>

Power JSONLoot table JSON (<code>example:blocks/minecraft/diamond_ore</code>) <pre><code>{
    "type": "neo-apoli:replace_loot_table",
    "replacements": {
        "([a-z0-9\\-_]+):blocks/([a-z0-9\\-_/]+)": "example:blocks/$1/$2"
    }
}
</code></pre> <pre><code>{
    "type": "minecraft:block",
    "pools": [
        {
            "rolls": 1.0,
            "entries": [
                {
                    "type": "minecraft:item",
                    "name": "minecraft:diamond_block"
                }
            ]
        }
    ]
}
</code></pre> <p>This example will replace loot tables of any blocks with loot tables created with the naming scheme: <code>example:blocks/&lt;namespace&gt;/&lt;path&gt;</code>. In the provided loot table example, it will replace the diamonds dropped by diamond ores with a diamond block.</p>

